  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Vinod Kumar vs State Of Haryana on 16 December, 2020 Author: Alka Sarin Bench: Alka Sarin 201 In The High Court Of Punjab And Haryana At Chandigarh -.- Crm-M-35866-2020 (O&M) Date of decision : 16.12.2020. Vinod Kumar .....Petitioner Vs. State of Haryana .....Respondent Coram: Hon'Ble Mrs. Justice Alka Sarin Present: Mr. Vikram Singh, Advocate, for the petitioner. Mr. Naveen Singh Panwar, Dag, Haryana.",
    "facts": "",
    "arguments": "\"This is a petition under Section 438 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 for grant of anticipatory bail to the petitioner in FIR No.514 dated 05.09.2020 under Sections 272, 420 of Indian Penal Code, 1860 and Sections 50, 51, 52, 54 and 59 of the Food Safety and Standard Act, 2006 registered at Police Station Chandani Bagh, Panipat, District Panipat. Learned counsel for the petitioner has contended that the police has no jurisdiction to take cognizance in respect of offences that are alleged in view of Section 42 of the Food Safety and Standard Act, 2006. He further contends that there is a complete procedure which has been prescribed under Section 42 and without the procedure being followed, no cognizance of any offence having been committed under the Act can be taken. Learned counsel for the petitioner relies upon the orders passed by this Court in CRM-M- 43580-2020.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "\"This is a petition under Section 438 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 for grant of anticipatory bail to the petitioner in FIR No.514 dated 05.09.2020 under Sections 272, 420 of Indian Penal Code, 1860 and Sections 50, 51, 52, 54 and 59 of the Food Safety and Standard Act, 2006 registered at Police Station Chandani Bagh, Panipat, District Panipat. Learned counsel for the petitioner has contended that the police has no jurisdiction to take cognizance in respect of offences that are alleged in view of Section 42 of the Food Safety and Standard Act, 2006. He further contends that there is a complete procedure which has been prescribed under Section 42 and without the procedure being followed, no cognizance of any offence having been committed under the Act can be taken. Learned counsel for the petitioner relies upon the orders passed by this Court in CRM-M- 43580-2020.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
